            "Cirrhosis is the irreversible replacement of liver tissue by fibrous scar and regenerating nodules after years of injury. Blood cannot flow freely through the scarred organ, so pressure rises in the portal vein (<strong>portal hypertension</strong>) and blood is diverted through collateral veins — oesophageal and gastric varices, rectal veins, the umbilical veins of caput medusae — while the spleen enlarges and traps platelets. The remaining hepatocytes cannot make enough albumin and clotting factors, clear enough ammonia, oestrogen and drugs, or store enough glucose. In Ghana the causes, in order, are <strong>chronic hepatitis B</strong>, <strong>alcohol</strong> (akpeteshie, palm wine, 'bitters', beer in quantity), hepatitis C, and increasingly fatty liver disease from obesity and diabetes; <strong>aflatoxin</strong> in mouldy groundnuts and maize multiplies the cancer risk; schistosomal periportal fibrosis produces portal hypertension with a liver that still functions.",
